{"tool": "finish", "summary": "Added a per-feed `refresh_interval` (integer minutes, 0 = use the global interval).\n\n- Migration: new `feeds.refresh_interval int not null default 0` column, appended at the end of the migration list.\n- Model: `Feed.RefreshInterval` (`json:\"refresh_interval\"`) and `FeedModificationRequest.RefreshInterval *int`, applied in `Patch`.\n- Scheduler: `Feed.ScheduleNextCheck` uses the per-feed interval when it is > 0, overriding the computed global interval (including the global max cap and any Retry-After/TTL delay).\n- Storage: column read in the feed query builder and written in `CreateFeed` / `UpdateFeed`.\n- API: `PUT /v1/feeds/{feedID}` accepts `refresh_interval`; negative values are rejected by `ValidateFeedModification`.\n- Web UI: number input named `refresh_interval` in the network settings section of the feed edit form; an empty value means 0, a non-numeric or negative value shows a validation error.\n- Go client: `Feed.RefreshInterval` and `FeedModificationRequest.RefreshInterval`.\n- Translations: two new keys (label and error) added to all 24 locale files, with English text in every one \u2014 they are not translated.\n\nExisting build and tests pass (53 unit packages, 102 integration tests). The new behaviour itself is not covered by any test I ran.\n\nNot done:\n- Changing the interval does not reschedule an already-set `next_check_at`; the new value takes effect from the feed's next refresh.\n- The field is not accepted on feed creation (`POST /v1/feeds`), only on update.\n- When the feed has its own interval, a server's Retry-After / TTL delay is ignored for the next check; say if you would rather take the larger of the two.", "t": 1794461650.06}
{"tool": "_result", "name": "finish", "chars": 55, "t": 1794461650.06}
